Short answer: worth pursuing as a bounded experiment, not as the main line. The interesting part is real — exact integer offset hits from Pythagorean normals — but it is a side channel, not the core ornament system.
The mile-high map
Thread	What it actually solves	Maturity	Value to curve-lab	Verdict
Integer-handle parabolas	Exact lattice points and integer cubic handles	Working sandbox	Clean, inspectable geometry; possible “lattice mode” for ornaments/plotting	Pursue briefly
Integer offset hits	Exact rails through lattice points via Pythagorean normals	Just found; sparse but real	Beautiful exact examples; needs a family search to be more than a curiosity	Time-box it
Levien parallel Béziers	Given (d), fit a cubic rail to the exact offset	Mature, published	Production-quality offset drawing	Use it; don’t reverse-engineer it
Bounding parabolas	Cubic/cubic intersection, cusps, boolean path ops	Draft PR/research	Useful only if you need robust intersection/topology	Defer
Main ornament arc	Stem, two rails, endings, flowers, ornament grammar	The actual goal	Creates the artifact	Stay on it
Why the integer thing is genuinely interesting
The \(d=5\) hit is not a coincidence:
\[
\text{tangent}=(4,3),\qquad \text{normal}=(-3,4),\qquad |(-3,4)|=5
\]
At source \(x=24\), the normal is an integer vector, so the offset lands exactly on \((21,13)\) and \((27,5)\). Multiples \(d=10,20\) give more exact hits. That is a general mechanism: choose a source point with a Pythagorean tangent, then use the normal length as the offset distance. It can generate exact integer rails, not just isolated luck.
Why it is not the main line
- Integer handles are easy only at special widths: \(24n\) for \(x^2/64\), \(12n\) for \(x^2/24\), etc.
- Integer offset hits are much rarer than integer source points.
- The constraint can distort the ornament. Most attractive curves do not need integer coordinates.
- Levien’s fitted rail is real-valued; it does not preserve interior integer hits. The exact rail is the ground truth.
- The bounding-parabola work solves intersection, not integer construction. Porting it would be a large detour unless you need boolean path ops.
What I would do
Yes, but with an exit condition.
1. Spend a short, fixed pass on a Pythagorean-normal search:
- enumerate integer-handle parabolas,
- find integer tangents/normals,
- catalog the exact offset distances and hits.
2. If it produces a reusable family — not just \(d=5\) — add one or two to the offset catalogue as a lattice mode.
3. If it produces only a handful of curiosities, keep the sandbox page and stop.
Use Levien as the renderer for fitted rails. Use the exact rails when integer hits matter. Do not port the bounding-parabola prototype yet.
The honest verdict
This is worth a short, documented exploration because it is exact, visual, and fits the “integer grid” aesthetic. It is not worth becoming the next major architecture. The main arc — stem, rails, endings, ornament grammar — is still where the value is.
A good rule: if the integer work gives you a new family of ornament vocabulary, continue. If it only gives prettier diagrams of parabolas, stop after the sandbox.
