Two opposite jobs
1. Draw a known Euler spiral with cubics
This is what euler-parabola-fit.html does.
- The Euler spiral is the true curve.
- A cubic Bézier is fitted to it.
- The cubic is the drawing approximation.
So: not “an Euler curve fits a cubic.” The cubic fits the Euler curve.
2. Import a cubic into the engine
This also exists in the project, in spine.py / interpolate.design_from_spans.
- The cubic span is the input.
- A clothoid/Euler segment is fitted to it, matching endpoints and tangents.
- The result is a normal engine segment with curvature.
So the reverse direction exists, but it is a different operation.
What the page’s handle construction means
For each Euler segment, normalize the chord to a straight line of length 1.
At each end, draw a parabola:
- its vertex is one-third of the way along the chord;
- its arms open two-thirds of the chord sideways at the endpoint.
Then draw the tangent line from the endpoint. Where that tangent crosses the parabola is where the cubic’s handle goes.
That is the entire rule. The parabola is not approximating the curve. It is only a guide for placing the handle.
The page’s comparison says:
- this handle placement already is what the current cubic drawing uses;
- subdividing the Euler segment into \(n\) pieces and fitting one cubic per piece gives about \(n^5\) error reduction;
- measured slope is 4.78 symmetric, 4.87 asymmetric;
- arclength also converges.
So the page is validating the existing cubic drawing, not introducing a new fit.
Is the “seam” the “spine”?
No.
- Spine = the structural centerline. The exact path that carries the design: the p-triple chain, its nodes, headings, and rails. It is the thing.
- Seam = the handoff to drawing. The cubic Bézier spans and their joins that let the SVG writer draw the spine. It is the approximation/ink.
A useful picture:
spine:   exact centerline / chain / rails
seam:    cubic spans used to draw that centerline
The seam is called a seam because it is where two representations meet: curvature-based geometry on one side, cubic Bézier ink on the other.
The phrase “current cubic seam” just means: “the cubics we already use to draw each spine segment.” It is not a second spine, and it is not the exact geometry.
What the editor is doing
The editor is a free cubic toy:
- move the two endpoints;
- drag the two handles, which stay on the two guide parabolas;
- see the cubic and the endpoint parabolas.
It is not fitting an Euler spiral. It is showing the handle geometry behind the cubic drawing rule.
The offset curves on the page
They are just the current cubic shifted along its normal:
\[
O_\pm(t)=C(t)\pm d\,N(t)
\]
They are not Levien’s fitted offset cubics and not the exact Euler parallel. They are sampled normal offsets for visual comparison.
So, in one sentence:
The page shows an Euler spiral being drawn by a cubic, using the existing handle-on-a-parabola rule; the “seam” is the cubic drawing layer, while the “spine” is the exact centerline the design is built on.

