The green parabolas are not the Euler curve. They are the locus of possible handle positions as the endpoint tangent angle changes.
At the vertex, the handle is the familiar one-third point.
As the endpoint tangent turns, the handle slides along the parabola.
At the far ends, \(v=\pm 2/3\), the endpoint tangent is 90° to the chord.
A single cubic with 90° end tangents can loop, cusp, or bow wildly. That is outside the range where one cubic is a good Euler fit. The real Euler segment would need to be split into several spans there.
The editor is a free cubic toy: it constrains the handles to the guide parabolas, but it does not enforce “this cubic is an Euler fit.” The comparison panels use moderate endpoint tangents—about \(19^\circ/19^\circ\) symmetric and \(-9^\circ/48^\circ\) asymmetric—and subdivide; that is where the \(n^5\) measurements apply.
