I'm here. 53.13° is the 3-4-5 triangle angle:
\[
53.1301\ldots^\circ=\arctan\!\left(\frac43\right)
\]
and its complement is
\[
36.8699\ldots^\circ=\arctan\!\left(\frac34\right).
\]
The horizontal integer S is
\[
P0=(0,0),\quad P1=(3,-4),\quad P2=(7,4),\quad P3=(10,0).
\]
Its two control arms are
\[
(3,-4)\quad\text{and}\quad(3,-4),
\]
each of length \(5\). The chord is \(10=2\cdot5\). So each end is a 3-4-5 triangle: horizontal 3, vertical 4, arm 5. The tangent is a rational slope, which is why the handles land on integer coordinates.
What is actually working
It is not the Euler fit becoming exact. The integer curve is a cubic whose handles are integer because its tangent direction is a Pythagorean slope. The guide parabolas are a separate constraint; the integer handles sit off them by about \(0.8667\) units.
So the useful thing is:
integer handle direction = rational tangent slope = Pythagorean triangle.
The 53.13° value is not magic by itself; it is the angle where the control arms are integer 3-4-5 vectors.
Rotating it
Yes, you can rotate it and keep integers, but the rotation has to cooperate.
Rotate the horizontal S by 53.13° and scale by 5:
\[
P0=(0,0),\quad
P1=(25,0),\quad
P2=(5,40),\quad
P3=(30,40).
\]
Now the chord is \((30,40)\), length 50, on the 3-4-5 angle. The handles are still integers. The endpoint tangents are horizontal, so relative to the chord they are still \(-53.13°\).
Rotate by 36.87° instead and scale by 5:
\[
P0=(0,0),\quad
P1=(24,-7),\quad
P2=(16,37),\quad
P3=(40,30).
\]
The chord is \((40,30)\), also a 3-4-5 direction, and the handles are integers again. The absolute tangent angles change; the angle between handle and chord stays \(53.13°\).
In general:
- Rotate by 90°: integer lattice preserved.
- Rotate by a 3-4-5 angle: coordinates become rational with denominator 5; scaling by 5 makes them integer.
- Rotate by an arbitrary angle: coordinates generally become irrational, so no integer representation.
- Rotate by a 3125-circle angle: denominator 3125; scaling by 3125 clears it.
That is what the 3125 buttons represent: the twelve integer points on the radius-3125 circle give the rational lattice-friendly rotations.
The honest summary
At 53.13°:
- the handle arms are exact integer 3-4-5 vectors;
- the cubic is a valid integer-control approximation;
- the Euler guide is still approximate, with a real-valued residual;
- the measured one-piece error is about \(0.00657\), two-piece \(0.00430\).
So the thing that “works” is the integer lattice geometry of the handle arms, not the Euler fit becoming exact. The 53.13° case is a particularly clean lattice coincidence; the 3125 circle gives the larger set of rotations with the same kind of rational denominator.

