I'm here. Partially — Levien solves the offset, not the integer-hit selection.
What Levien already solves
Given a source curve and a distance \(d\), his method computes a fitted cubic offset rail:
- endpoint positions and tangents fixed
- area and first moment matched
- quartic candidates tested by Tiller–Hanson error
- subdivide if needed
That is already implemented in the page’s selectable cards through fit_chain. So we do not need a new offset algorithm.
What he does not solve
Levien takes \(d\) as an input. He does not:
- search for a \(d\) that makes the rail hit integer points,
- snap control points or rail points to integers,
- guarantee that the fitted cubic passes through an interior integer point.
The clean hit I found comes from the exact offset, not from the fit:
At source point \(x=24\) on \(y=x^2/64\):
\[
\text{tangent}=(4,3),\qquad \text{normal}=(-3,4)
\]
So a distance of 5 is exactly the integer normal length:
\[
(24,9)+(-3,4)=(21,13)
\]
and on the other rail:
\[
(24,9)+(3,-4)=(27,5)
\]
That is a \(3\!-\!4\!-\!5\) hit. Distances \(10\) and \(20\) scale it:
- \(d=10\): \((18,17)\) and \((30,1)\)
- \(d=20\): \((12,25)\) and \((36,-7)\)
For \(d=2\), the clean hit is only the vertex \((0,\pm2)\). That is why the gallery now uses \(5/10/20\), not 2.
The Levien fit can share a hit when it is an endpoint of the fitted segment — as in \(0,24\) at \(d=5\), where the endpoint must match. For interior points in the longer strips, it is an approximation, not a guarantee.
