Semicubical vs cubic Bézier cusp: yes, they are the same cusp family. The Neile parabola is y² = x³, the evolute of the parabola, and every ordinary cusp is locally y² = x³. The symmetric X
P₀=(−1,−1), P₁=(1,1), P₂=(−1,1), P₃=(1,−1) has B′(½)=0, so it is a cusp, not a loop, and is a cuspidal cubic — the semicubical up to an affine change of coordinates. The canonical exact control points for x=t², y=t³ are P₀=P₁=(0,0), P₂=(1/3,0), P₃=(1,1). The Tschirnhausen cubic is the different, nodal PH cubic (node angle 60°); a symmetric-X Bézier with non-zero velocity at the crossing is a node, not the semicubical cusp.
