This is classical — the names
A line touching a curve at two points is a bitangent or double tangent; if the curve lies on one side, it is a double supporting line.
Counting them: Fabricius–Bjerre for closed curves.
Rotating the curve/box to find them: rotating calipers, antipodal pairs, minimum-width / minimum-area enclosing rectangle.
The physical “rests on two points” version: stable support, supporting segment, resting pose.
References
Bitangent: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitangent
Rotating calipers / antipodal pairs: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotating_calipers
Fabricius–Bjerre double tangents: https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~webfiles/undergrad_thesis/201403_Emily_Macway_Thompson.pdf
Minimum width and antipodal pairs (with applet): https://sarielhp.org/research/CG/applets/bounding_rectangle/main.html
Bitangents of parametric curves / convex hull: https://doi.org/10.1145/986537.986590
Numerical bitangent algorithm: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/61295307/algorithm-to-find-the-bitangent-of-a-2d-curve
Resting bodies / stable two-contact pose: https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~kmcrane/Projects/RestingBodies/PuttingRigidBodiesToRest.pdf
Two frictionless contacts: https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~me/papers/IROS95-abell-abs.html
Supporting segment / polygon equilibrium: https://www.codingame.com/training/medium/polygon-equilibrium
Curve vs box
Rotating the box is the rotating-calipers method: two parallel support lines, each touching once. The minimum width is an antipodal pair (a double normal).
Rotating the curve on a table is a single support line touching twice — a bitangent. Both come out of the same support-line sweep, but they are different contact pairs.
For a smooth curve, solve the bitangent system numerically; for a polyline, use the convex hull and rotating calipers.
So the cloud join should use the full ± near-bitangent at 38.5° CW (gap 0.0003), not the half-axis 0.079. I’ll correct the wording on the heart/clothoid pages and add the full-curve contact figure.

