I'm here. Your table picture is right — and it’s exactly the distinction.
A tangent is easy. Every smooth point of the clothoid has one. Lay the curve on a table and the table touches the lowest point once. Slide or rotate it and the contact moves. That is a normal, single tangent.
A double tangent is stricter. It means one single straight line touching the curve at two different points at the same time — like laying a ruler across the two humps of a heart. That is what the mirror join needs: the mirror line must be tangent at both ends of the arc, so the reflected half leaves in exactly the same direction. If the line touches only one end, the other end meets it at a corner.
Laying the whole spiral on a table gives the table contact at one point, not two. The table is a plane; in cross-section it is one support line. It does not rest on a second turn as well.
Why the clothoid fails. As the spiral winds in, the points where the tangent is vertical crowd toward the eye. The best you can do is put a vertical line through one of them and see how far the other one is:
- 2 turns: 0.079 units apart
- 4 turns: 0.0216
- 8 turns: 0.0069
- 16 turns: 0.0023
The gap shrinks, but only reaches zero in the limit as the spiral closes forever. For any finite arc there is a small gap, so the mirror line is tangent at one end and short of the other. (And the pair above is the closest pair, near the eye — a heart-sized pair is farther apart still.)
The bounding box is a different thing. Its left and right walls are two parallel rulers, each touching once. They are parallel, but they are not the same ruler. That is why the box gives a “double normal” — two facing contact points with parallel tangents — and not the double tangent you want.
So for the cloud you have three honest options:
1. place two separate arcs at each join, each tangent to the join line at its own endpoint;
2. accept a stated small gap (0.079 at 2 turns, smaller with more turns);
3. use a different curve/form whose two tangencies line up exactly.
The table gives you a natural single tangent. The cloud join needs a shared tangent at two points.
