2. The Euler match — and it splits the family cleanly
New page: workbench/euler-match.html, one figure per curve, in the hub.
Three chains compared, because they answer different questions:
chain	result
Euler polygon (heading from the curve at each node)	under 1% on all nineteen, 16–512 chords
one clothoid (single quadratic heading)	fails: 35% bean, 45% nodal, 72% Bernoulli
chain of clothoid spans	0.44–1.1% at 4–32 spans on ten curves
Why one clothoid fails is the useful part. A clothoid has one linear-curvature law; these curves reverse curvature — the bean, the lemniscate, the nodal cubic all bend both ways. One laW cannot follow a sign change. Split into spans and it works.
What no clothoid can do is a cusp. The astroid, deltoid, cardioid, nephroid and the epicycloids keep 9–63% no matter how many spans you spend, because a clothoid turns finitely and the chain cuts the corner. Those want a joint, not a blend — which is Segment.corner, already in the engine.
3. Where that leaves your plan
Your instinct — Euler-like segments for smoothness — is right, with one amendment:
- smooth curves (bean, nodal, Tschirnhausen, trifolium, quadrifolium, Gerono, piriform, Bernoulli, circle, rounded square): clothoid spans at ~0.5–1%, 4–32 spans, on their own grids.
- cusped roulettes: they need the corner machinery. Fitting them with clothoids is the wrong model, not a tuning problem.
The remaining number to chase is the grid snap — I report it per curve, and it's counter-intuitive: a smaller grid is worse. Snapping onto grid 1 moves nodes up to 0.5 units; onto grid 273 it moves them 0.05. If you want the joints on integers, the fine grids are your friend.
That's the natural next step: take the span boundaries and place them on the curve's grid, then re-measure — the cost of lattice-locking the clothoid chain.

