I'm here. Yes for smoothness — but it costs you the integers, and that's the whole decision. Let me be concrete.
Why clothoids are the right tool for "super smooth"
A Bezier chain joins with a matching direction (G1). The curvature still jumps at each joint — small in the drawing, visible when you run a machine along it. Clothoid segments join with matching curvature (G2), which is what "no wobble" actually means. That's the reason highway ramps are clothoids and not arcs.
So your instinct is right: if smoothness is the goal, clothoids are the correct segment.
The catch
A clothoid's points are transcendental — they can't be written as fractions. Which means:
- not one intermediate point has integer coordinates. Every hit we just spent the afternoon finding disappears.
- You keep integers only at the joints, and only if you choose to place them there.
So the two things we've been building pull in opposite directions:
goal	tool	integers
land on the grid	Bezier / PH	yes, everywhere
no curvature jumps	clothoid	only where you deliberately place a joint
What the repo already knows about this
This isn't new ground — it's the spine layer's existing job:
- one clothoid fitted per recovered cubic span, 586 spans, median error 0.00–0.03% of the chord;
- the practical curve: 34 cubics at 0.42 mm worst deviation on a 200 × 250 sheet;
- the accepted fidelity budget is 1.4–1.6% of study height.
So "match with clothoid segments" will work. The open question isn't whether, it's what it costs in segments.
How I'd sequence it
Don't replace the integer curves — run the two layers side by side, which is what the project already does elsewhere:
1. Lattice layer — the integer Bezier curves, one grid each, as now. This is what lands on ticks.
2. Smooth layer — a clothoid chain fitted to the same silhouette, G2 at every joint. This is what gets cut, offset, and looks right at size.
The useful measurement between them is: for each named curve, how many clothoid segments does it take to stay inside the fidelity budget, and where do the joints land? If the joints can be snapped to integer nodes without adding segments, you get both at once. If they can't, you've learned which curves are worth the compromise.
