 Nineteen curves, and the epicycloid turned out to be the family that explains half of them.
The epicycloid, and what it revealed
k is the only parameter: a circle of radius r rolling outside one of radius R = kr traces
x = r(k+1)cos θ − r cos((k+1)θ)
y = r(k+1)sin θ − r sin((k+1)θ)
k = 1 → cardioid — already on the page
k = 2 → nephroid — already on the page
k = 3 → four cusps, degree 8 — added, control scale 35
k = 5 → six cusps, degree 12 — added, control scale 231
Degree climbs as 2(k+1), and every integer k stays rational, because cos(mθ) is a Chebyshev polynomial in cos θ — so tan(θ/2) rationalises the whole family at any degree. I wrote the generator generally, so any k you name is a one-line addition rather than a hand-computed polynomial list.
Worth noting: the cardioid and nephroid on the page use their own normalisations, so k = 1 doesn't coincide with the cardioid card — same curve, different origin and scale. That's why I added k = 3 and k = 5 rather than duplicating k = 1.
